{"abstract":"Stein variational gradient descent (SVGD) (Liu & Wang, 2016) performs approximate Bayesian inference by representing the posterior with a set of particles. However, SVGD suffers from variance collapse, i.e. poor predictions due to underestimating uncertainty (Ba et al., 2021), even for moderately-dimensional models such as small Bayesian neural networks (BNNs). To address this issue, we generalize SVGD by letting each particle parameterize a component distribution in a mixture model. Our method, Stein Mixture Inference (SMI), optimizes a lower bound to the evidence (ELBO) and introduces user-specified guides parameterized by particles. SMI extends the Nonlinear SVGD framework (Wang & Liu, 2019) to the case of variational Bayes. SMI effectively avoids variance collapse, judging by a previously described test developed for this purpose, and performs well on standard data sets. In addition, SMI requires considerably fewer particles than SVGD to accurately estimate uncertainty for small BNNs. The synergistic combination of NSVGD, ELBO optimization and user-specified guides establishes a promising approach towards variational Bayesian inference in the case of tall and wide data.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Rønning, Ola","Nalisnick, Eric","Ley, Christophe","Smyth, Padhraic","Hamelryck, Thomas Wim"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-828523"],"event":{"end":"2025-04-28","place":"Singapore, sg","start":"2025-04-24","title":"13th International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR 2025","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Luxembourg","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of Computer Science","Johns Hopkins University","University of Luxembourg"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"elboing stein variational bayes with stein mixture inference","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/457732910/ELBOING_STEIN.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-828523","identifier":[{"id":"105010187810","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-259afd1f-d87e-428b-a68d-e84eff2b22a8"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-7ACF168D357EF22536CD179E24C52A71"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=2rBLbNJwBm","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/457732910/ELBOING_STEIN.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Programming Languages and Theory of Computation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"johns hopkins university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Johns Hopkins University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"lu","key":"university of luxembourg","loc":["Luxembourg","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Luxembourg","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"department of computer science","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Department of Computer Science","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ola","key":"rønningpau","last":"Rønning","locid":"3cf9dc40-061c-4c7a-9beb-167c5cbee058","name":"Rønning, Ola","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Eric","key":"nalisnickpau","last":"Nalisnick","name":"Nalisnick, Eric","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Christophe","key":"leypau","last":"Ley","name":"Ley, Christophe","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Padhraic","key":"smythpau","last":"Smyth","name":"Smyth, Padhraic","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas","key":"hamelryckpau","last":"Hamelryck","locid":"ef00d7fc-4441-4410-9674-7bb5d0c17b8c","name":"Hamelryck, Thomas Wim","name_org":"Hamelryck, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0003-2917-3602","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9798331320850"],"pages":"4709-4733","publisher":"International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR","status":"p","title":"13th International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR 2025"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010187810"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-259afd1f-d87e-428b-a68d-e84eff2b22a8"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"ELBOing Stein : Variational Bayes with Stein Mixture Inference","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-828523","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This action research article presents a case study of a global manufacturing company deploying artificial intelligence (AI) to develop capabilities and enhance decision-making. This study explores considerations and trade-offs involved in introducing AI into daily operations, leading up to the decision to develop AI capabilities in-house or outsource them. The case study offers in-depth technical descriptions of model selection, dataset creation, model adoption, model training and evaluation while addressing organizational obstacles and decision-making processes. The study’s findings highlight the importance of collaboration between technical experts, business leaders, and end-users, as well as the interaction and collaboration between AI systems and human employees in the workplace. The article contributes a practical perspective on AI implementation in manufacturing, emphasizing the need to balance in-house capability development with external acquisition. Although the case study company managed to create an in-house model, factors such as implementation, debugging, data requirements, training time, and performance led to outsourcing the capabilities. However, making this informed decision required capabilities and insights that were acquired through practical work. Consequently, although in-house development can be challenging, it can also enhance organizational capabilities and provide the necessary knowledge to make informed decisions about future development or outsourcing.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Eklöf, Jon","Snis, Ulrika Lundh","Hamelryck, Thomas Wim","Grima, Alexander","Rønning, Ola"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-731840"],"event":{"end":"2024-01-06","place":"Hawaii, us","start":"2024-01-03","title":"57th Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences - HICSS-57","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","GKN Aerospace","University West"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"ai implementation and capability development in manufacturing an action research case","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/384068822/AI_Implementation.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-731840","ids":["ku-942d5bf2-659f-40a7-a1a7-8c3af17e1db6"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6DFA275B8E56A0221EA006D2F1F80009"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/384068822/AI_Implementation.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Programming Languages and Theory of Computation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"gkn aerospace","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"GKN Aerospace","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"university west","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University West","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Jon","key":"eklöfpau","last":"Eklöf","locid":"f75c4c10-8a64-4b59-8c80-635b52494c3f","name":"Eklöf, Jon","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Ulrika Lundh","key":"snispau","last":"Snis","name":"Snis, Ulrika Lundh","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas Wim","key":"hamelryckpau","last":"Hamelryck","locid":"ef00d7fc-4441-4410-9674-7bb5d0c17b8c","name":"Hamelryck, Thomas Wim","orcid":"0000-0003-2917-3602","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Alexander","key":"grimapau","last":"Grima","name":"Grima, Alexander","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ola","key":"rønningpau","last":"Rønning","locid":"3cf9dc40-061c-4c7a-9beb-167c5cbee058","name":"Rønning, Ola","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 57th Hawaii International Conference on System Sciences - HICSS 2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-942d5bf2-659f-40a7-a1a7-8c3af17e1db6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"AI Implementation and Capability Development in Manufacturing: An Action Research Case","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-731840","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Particle-based variational inference (ParVI) methods are a powerful class of Bayesian inferences algorithms due to their flexible and accurate approximations. ParVI can interpolate between Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) and variational inference (VI) methods, and, as such, ParVI is a suitable candidate for a universal black box inference engine. Bayesian statistical inference is a cornerstone of the empirical sciences. However, correctly realizing the algorithms for Bayesian inference is notoriously tricky and should be left to experts. Universal black box inference engines (and their associated probabilistic programming language (PPL)) enable the separation of model specification from statistical inference (data conditioning). This separation allows scientists to formulate statistical hypotheses (probabilistic graphical models) as stochastic (probabilistic) computer programs without implementing the inference algorithm. The black-box nature of the inference algorithm ensures the PPL can condition the probabilistic program on experimental observations automatically. Modern PPLs are generally designed for either MCMC or VI inference, requiring translation to another PPL for an unbiased comparison. Tranlation introduces unnecessary overhead and the potential for code drift–something we can wholly avoid with a generalizing framework like ParVI. An auspicious ParVI method is Stein variational gradient descent (Liu and Wang, 2016a) (SVGD) due to its direct connection with Stein’s method, gradient flows and reproducing kernels. These connections make SVGD versatile and well-suited for theoretical analysis, yielding significant results regarding SVGDs convergence, kernel choice, and convergence rates. However, the adoption of SVGD by practitioners could be more extensive. This is partly due to insufficient tooling and a lack of a mature set of best practices for hyper-parameter choice. In the first part of this thesis, we extend Stein mixture (SM) (an SVGD variate) to a whole class of approximate inference algorithms indexed by a scalar. We recommend the best choice of indexing scalar and demonstrate why by analyzing the gradient noise. We also present a ready-to-use library for inference with SM as an extension to the NumPyro PPL. We call the library EinStein, which includes the black box SM inference engine, automatic guide generation, many studied kernels, and copiable examples of Bayesian neural network (Neal, 2012) (BNN)s and Deep Markov model (Wu et al., 2018) (DMM)s. In the second part of the thesis, we study the protein structure prediction problem as a showcase for applying PPLs in the natural sciences. The protein prediction problem aims to predict the (ensemble of) conformation( s) a particular protein may adopt(s) given its sequence of amino acids (and potentially known protein homologs). A high-fidelity solution to the problem could have a massive impact on treatment for misfolding diseases such as cancer, Alzheimer’s, Huntington’s, and Parkinson’s. A canonical representation of a protein conformation is its internal (toroidal) coordinates. Internal coordinates allow efficient updates to the protein’s three-dimensional structure without violating physiochemical properties. To infer statistical models over internal coordinate representations, we introduce a variate of the bivariate von Mises distribution (a 2-torus distribution) in the (Num)Pyro PPLs. The distribution (known as the sine distribution) enables us to specify a hierarchical model over the two high-variance backbone torsion angles. Our model captures probable angle pairs for each amino acid order of magnitude faster than preexisting methods. Finally, we present our preliminary results on inferring a distribution over protein folding forcefields. Current technologies for protein structure prediction are excellent at the single-structure forecast. However, these methods are black box deep models and yield no insights into physiochemical properties–sometimes even violating them. Our formulation of the folding force as a probabilistic program allows us to automate the other tedious process of tuning protein folding forcefields using our SM inference engine. We can incorporate the existing (known) hyperparameters by choice of prior. SM ability to capture rich correlations in the parameter space makes it a suitable statistical inference algorithm for these forcefields, which tend to be highly sensitive to parameterization. Presuming our forcefield can fold (small to medium size proteins) proteins, the parameter distributions will yield insights into the importance (and sensitivity) of the different (potential) energy terms of the forcefield and a high-resolution view of the aggregate folding trajectory of the protein.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Rønning, Ola"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-701891"],"filters":{"added":"2023-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a probabilistic approach to the protein folding problem using stein based variational inference","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"do","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-701891","ids":["ku-7a4375d1-9be4-4264-a184-1bdc65127577"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B3230F1B83B1509D794A7F6ECFDDB4F7"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Programming Languages and Theory of Computation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ola","key":"rønningpau","last":"Rønning","locid":"3cf9dc40-061c-4c7a-9beb-167c5cbee058","name":"Rønning, Ola","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"129","publisher":"Department of Computer Science, Faculty of Science, University of Copenhagen","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-7a4375d1-9be4-4264-a184-1bdc65127577"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Probabilistic Approach to the Protein Folding Problem : Using Stein-based Variational Inference","type":"Other","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-701891","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"We present a technique for applying reverse mode automatic differentiation (AD) on a non-recursive second-order functional array language that supports nested parallelism and is primarily aimed at efficient GPU execution. The key idea is to eliminate the need for a tape by relying on redundant execution to bring into each new scope all program variables that may be needed by the differentiated code. Efficient execution is enabled by the observation that perfectly nested scopes do not introduce re-execution and that such perfect nests can be readily produced by application of known compiler transformations. Our technique differentiates loops and bulk-parallel operators-e.g., map, reduce(-by-index), scan, and scatter-by specific rewrite rules and aggressively optimizes the resulting nested-parallel code. We report an evaluation that compares with established AD solutions and demonstrates competitive performance on ten common benchmarks from recent applied AD literature.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Schenck, Robert","Rønning, Ola","Henriksen, Troels","Oancea, Cosmin Eugen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-669699"],"event":{"end":"2022-11-18","place":"Dallas, us","start":"2022-11-13","title":"2022 International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis, SC 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Automatic differentiation","Compilers","GPGPU"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"ad for an array language with nested parallelism","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/345320951/AD_for_an_Array_Language_with_Nested_Parallelism.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-669699","identifier":[{"id":"85149327504","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-06fcc791-5584-4716-a9c1-0e95f82d16f0"],"keyword":["GPGPU","automatic differentiation","compilers","functional data parallel language"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6801E309F2833337FA6032963C7F31E9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/345320951/AD_for_an_Array_Language_with_Nested_Parallelism.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Programming Languages and Theory of Computation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Robert","key":"schenckpau","last":"Schenck","locid":"3c2a19e9-3075-4ba3-80aa-b330c5029847","name":"Schenck, Robert","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ola","key":"rønningpau","last":"Rønning","locid":"3cf9dc40-061c-4c7a-9beb-167c5cbee058","name":"Rønning, Ola","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Troels","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"7a9aebcb-498d-4266-b7bb-bd83410fba62","name":"Henriksen, Troels","orcid":"0000-0002-1195-9722","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Cosmin E.","key":"oanceapau","last":"Oancea","locid":"2484b4d6-cb0a-4ce9-bf3e-95ffd9755950","name":"Oancea, Cosmin Eugen","name_org":"Oancea, Cosmin E.","orcid":"0000-0001-5421-6876","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/sc41404.2022.00063","eisbn":["9781665454445"],"issn":["21674329"],"publisher":"IEEE Computer Society Press","series":"International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis, SC","status":"p","subtitle":"International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis","title":"Proceedings of SC 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85149327504"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-06fcc791-5584-4716-a9c1-0e95f82d16f0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"AD for an Array Language with Nested Parallelism","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-669699","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Probabilistic programming languages (PPLs) are at the interface between statistics and the theory of programming languages. PPLs formulate statistical models as stochastic programs that enable automatic inference algorithms and optimization. Pyro [1] and its sibling NumPyro [2] are PPLs built on top of the deep learning frameworks PyTorch [3] and Jax [4], respectively. Both PPLs provide simple, highly similar interfaces for inference using efficient implementations of Hamiltonian Monte Carlo (HMC), the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS), and Stochastic Variational Inference (SVI). They automatically generate variational distributions from a model, automatically enumerate discrete variables, and support formulating deep probabilistic models such as variational autoencoders and deep Markov models. The Sine von Mises distribution and its skewed variant are toroidal distributions relevant to protein bioinformatics. They provide a natural way to model the dihedral angles of protein structures, which is important in protein structure prediction, simulation and analysis. We present efficient implementations of the Sine von Mises distribution and its skewing in Pyro and NumPyro, and devise a simulation method that increases efficiency with several orders of magnitude when using parallel hardware (i.e., modern CPUs, GPUs, and TPUs). We demonstrate the use of the skewed Sine von Mises distribution by modeling dihedral angles of proteins using a Bayesian mixture model inferred using NUTS, exploiting NumPyro's facilities for automatic enumeration [5].","author_count":4,"contributors":["Rønning, Ola","Ley, Christophe","Mardia, Kanti V.","Hamelryck, Thomas Wim"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-395264"],"event":{"end":"2021-09-25","place":"Karlsruhe, de","start":"2021-09-23","title":"2021 IEEE International Conference on Multisensor Fusion and Integration for Intelligent Systems, MFI 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ghent University","University of Leeds"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"time efficient bayesian inference for a skewed von mises distribution on the torus in a deep probabilistic programming language","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85122864465&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-395264","identifier":[{"id":"85122864465","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-03033f55-61cc-49de-91b3-d8f8f7c21f41"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AF593E93C033BC1A6F618263D0C48CE0"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Programming Languages and Theory of Computation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"ghent university","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ghent University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of leeds","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Leeds","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ola","key":"ronningpau","last":"Ronning","locid":"3cf9dc40-061c-4c7a-9beb-167c5cbee058","name":"Rønning, Ola","name_org":"Ronning, Ola","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Christophe","key":"leypau","last":"Ley","name":"Ley, Christophe","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Kanti V.","key":"mardiapau","last":"Mardia","name":"Mardia, Kanti V.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas","key":"hamelryckpau","last":"Hamelryck","locid":"ef00d7fc-4441-4410-9674-7bb5d0c17b8c","name":"Hamelryck, Thomas Wim","name_org":"Hamelryck, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0003-2917-3602","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/mfi52462.2021.9591184","eisbn":["9781665445214"],"pages":"1-8","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2021 IEEE International Conference on Multisensor Fusion and Integration for Intelligent Systems (MFI)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85122864465"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-03033f55-61cc-49de-91b3-d8f8f7c21f41"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Time-efficient Bayesian Inference for a (Skewed) von Mises Distribution on the Torus in a Deep Probabilistic Programming Language","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-395264","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Sluice resolution in English is the problem of finding antecedents of wh-fronted ellipses. Previous work has relied on handcrafted features over syntax trees that scale poorly to other languages and domains; in particular, to dialogue, which is one of the most interesting applications of sluice resolution. Syntactic information is arguably important for sluice resolution, but we show that multi-task learning with partial parsing as auxiliary tasks effectively closes the gap and buys us an additional 9% error reduction over previous work. Since we are not directly relying on features from partial parsers, our system is more robust to domain shifts, giving a 26% error reduction on embedded sluices in dialogue.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Rønning, Ola","Hardt, Daniel","Søgaard, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-295579"],"event":{"acronym":"NAACL HLT","end":"2018-06-06","place":"New Orleans, us","start":"2018-06-01","title":"The 16th Annual Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sluice resolution without hand crafted features over brittle syntax trees","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58519593/N18_2038.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-295579","identifier":[{"id":"85081750529","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-d43ae962-a226-4b32-a45a-71caecc5e178"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-151A91EFA4553490CE95148B3D96B1AA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58519593/N18_2038.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"http://aclweb.org/anthology/N18-2","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Digitalization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ola","key":"rønningpau","last":"Rønning","name":"Rønning, Ola","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"hardtpau","last":"Hardt","locid":"afd9ce14-fa22-401a-8e3c-964ed4b0eb30","name":"Hardt, Daniel","orcid":"0000-0001-6041-1475","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"søgaardpau","last":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Stephanie","key":"lukinpedh","last":"Lukin","name":"Lukin, Stephanie","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Margaret","key":"mitchellpedh","last":"Mitchell","name":"Mitchell, Margaret","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9781948087292"],"pages":"236-241","place":"New Orleans, LA","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","subtitle":"Human Language Technologies","title":"Proceedings of the 2018 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85081750529"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-d43ae962-a226-4b32-a45a-71caecc5e178"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Sluice Resolution without Hand-Crafted Features over Brittle Syntax Trees","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-295579","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Sluicing resolution is the task of identifyingthe antecedent to a question ellipsis. Antecedentsare often sentential constituents, andprevious work has therefore relied on syntacticparsing, together with complex linguisticfeatures. A recent model instead used partialparsing as an auxiliary task in sequential neuralnetwork architectures to inject syntactic information.We explore the linguistic informationbeing brought to bear by such networks,both by defining subsets of the data exhibitingrelevant linguistic characteristics, and byexamining the internal representations of thenetwork. Both perspectives provide evidencefor substantial linguistic knowledge being deployedby the neural networks.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Rønning, Ola","Hardt, Daniel","Søgaard, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-476284"],"event":{"end":"2018-11-01","place":"Brussels, be","start":"2018-11-01","title":"2018 EMNLP Workshop BlackboxNLP","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Cobiro"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"linguistic representations in multi task neural networks for ellipsis resolution","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-476284","ids":["ku-8a949150-79df-42af-a874-7ef2fbd942f5"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3758F229A1BA9A665620E73E8048CC86"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Cobiro"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Machine Learning"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ola","key":"rønningpau","last":"Rønning","name":"Rønning, Ola","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Daniel","key":"hardtpau","last":"Hardt","name":"Hardt, Daniel","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Anders","key":"søgaardpau","last":"Søgaard","locid":"5cfea506-104a-4b40-b63b-e29d037159b0","name":"Søgaard, Anders","orcid":"0000-0001-5250-4276","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"66-73","publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p","subtitle":"Analyzing and Interpreting Neural Networks for NLP","title":"Proceedings of the 2018 EMNLP Workshop BlackboxNLP"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8a949150-79df-42af-a874-7ef2fbd942f5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Linguistic representations in multi-task neural networks for ellipsis resolution","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-476284","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Sluicing resolution is the task of identifying the antecedent to a question ellipsis. Antecedents are often sentential constituents, and previous work has therefore relied on syntactic parsing, together with complex linguistic features. A recent model instead used partial parsing as an auxiliary task in sequential neural network architectures to inject syntactic information. We explore the linguistic information being brought to bear by such networks, both by deﬁning subsets of the data exhibiting relevant linguistic characteristics, and by examining the internal representations of the network. Both perspectives provide evidence for substantial linguistic knowledge being deployed by the neural networks.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Rønning, Ola","Hardt, Daniel","Søgaard, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-293640"],"event":{"acronym":"EMNLP","end":"2018-11-04","place":"Brussels, be","start":"2018-10-31","title":"2018 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Cobiro"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"linguistic representations in multi task neural networks for ellipsis resolution","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58519085/R_nning_Hardt_S_gaard.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-293640","identifier":[{"id":"85070349602","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-c02eca9a-86d5-4a72-abaf-7b228d81ea62"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-3557E1BB557B8F4A75E5263D9FA84914"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","licens